========================================================
1. PYTHON STRINGS
========================================================

A string is a sequence of characters enclosed inside
single quotes or double quotes.

Strings are used to store and work with text.

Example:

name = "Niranjan"
city = "Bangalore"

print(name)
print(city)


Output:

Niranjan
Bangalore


Strings can contain:

- Letters
- Numbers
- Spaces
- Symbols
- Special characters


Examples:

name = "Niranjan"
number = "12345"
message = "Hello Python!"
email = "niranjan@gmail.com"
password = "Python@123"


Even though 12345 contains only numbers, "12345" is
treated as a string because it is enclosed inside quotes.


CHECKING STRING TYPE:

name = "Niranjan"

print(type(name))


Output:

<class 'str'>


The str means string.


SINGLE QUOTES:

name = 'Niranjan'

print(name)


DOUBLE QUOTES:

name = "Niranjan"

print(name)


Both single quotes and double quotes can be used to
create strings.


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES:

username = "niranjan"
password = "Python@123"
email = "niranjan@gmail.com"
website = "https://www.google.com"


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

Strings are used whenever a program needs to work
with text.

Strings are commonly used for:

- Names
- Usernames
- Passwords
- Email addresses
- Messages
- Addresses
- URLs
- File names
- Text data


========================================================
2. MULTILINE STRINGS
========================================================

Python allows us to create strings that contain
multiple lines.

Triple quotes can be used for multiline strings.

Example:

message = """Hello,
My name is Niranjan.
I am learning Python."""

print(message)


Output:

Hello,
My name is Niranjan.
I am learning Python.


We can also use triple single quotes:

message = '''Hello,
I am learning Python.
Python is interesting.'''

print(message)


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

address = """Niranjan Kumar
Bangalore
Karnataka
India"""

print(address)


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

Multiline strings are useful when storing paragraphs,
messages, addresses, documentation, and other large
blocks of text.


========================================================
3. STRINGS AS ARRAYS
========================================================

Strings in Python can be treated as sequences of
characters.

Each character has a position called an index.

Example:

word = "Python"

print(word[0])
print(word[1])
print(word[2])


Output:

P
y
t


The indexing starts from 0.


String:

P  y  t  h  o  n
0  1  2  3  4  5


Example:

language = "Python"

print(language[0])
print(language[3])
print(language[5])


Output:

P
h
n


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

Every character in a string has an index number.

Python starts counting indexes from 0.


========================================================
4. NEGATIVE INDEXING
========================================================

Python also supports negative indexing.

Negative indexing starts from the end of the string.


Example:

word = "Python"

print(word[-1])
print(word[-2])
print(word[-3])


Output:

n
o
h


String:

P   y   t   h   o   n
-6 -5  -4  -3  -2  -1


The last character can be accessed using -1.


Example:

name = "Niranjan"

print(name[-1])


Output:

n


REAL-WORLD USE:

Negative indexing is useful when we want to access
characters from the end of a string.

For example, checking the last character of a filename
or accessing the last character of a username.


========================================================
5. STRING SLICING
========================================================

String slicing is used to extract a part of a string.

Syntax:

string[start:end]


The start index is included.

The end index is not included.


Example:

text = "Python"

print(text[0:3])


Output:

Pyt


Indexes:

P  y  t  h  o  n
0  1  2  3  4  5


text[0:3] means:

Start at index 0
Stop before index 3


ANOTHER EXAMPLE:

text = "Hello World"

print(text[0:5])


Output:

Hello


SLICING WITH DIFFERENT POSITIONS:

text = "Python Programming"

print(text[0:6])
print(text[7:18])


Output:

Python
Programming


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

Slicing allows us to extract specific portions of
a string without changing the original string.


========================================================
6. SLICING FROM THE BEGINNING
========================================================

We can leave the starting index empty.

Example:

text = "Python"

print(text[:4])


Output:

Pyth


This is the same as:

print(text[0:4])


Another example:

text = "Programming"

print(text[:7])


Output:

Program


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

If the starting index is omitted, Python starts
from index 0.


========================================================
7. SLICING TO THE END
========================================================

We can leave the ending index empty.

Example:

text = "Python"

print(text[2:])


Output:

thon


This means:

Start from index 2 and continue until the end.


Another example:

text = "Python Programming"

print(text[7:])


Output:

Programming


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

If the ending index is omitted, Python continues
until the end of the string.


========================================================
8. NEGATIVE SLICING
========================================================

Negative indexes can also be used for slicing.


Example:

text = "Python"

print(text[-3:])


Output:

hon


Another example:

text = "Python"

print(text[:-2])


Output:

Pyth


REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:

filename = "document.pdf"

extension = filename[-3:]

print(extension)


Output:

pdf


WHAT I UNDERSTOOD:

Negative slicing is useful when working with the
end portion of strings such as file extensions,
codes, IDs, and other text.


